        {/* WRAPPING IT UP SECTION */}
        <section id="wrapping-it-up" style="background: var(--bg-card, #ffffff); border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 16px; padding: 24px; margin-bottom: 32px; text-align: center; width: 100%; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.03);">
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 10px 0; color: #2A680D;">Wrapping It Up</h2>
          <p style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #09090b; margin-bottom: 16px; max-width: 850px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
            A SIEM, stripped of marketing language, is really a straightforward idea: pull security relevant data together from everywhere it lives, make it speak a common language, and watch for patterns that suggest something worth a human's attention is happening. Where the category has struggled for two decades is the assumption that generating the alert was the hard part. It never was. The hard part, the part that determines whether an organization catches a real intrusion or buries it under a pile of alerts nobody had time to properly work, has always been the investigation that follows.
          </p>
          <p style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #09090b; margin-bottom: 16px; max-width: 850px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
            That is the exact gap 24Observe's approach to SIEM and detections was built to close: eighty seven prebuilt detections across fourteen packs covering authentication abuse, data exfiltration, secret exposure, cloud control plane tampering, and dedicated AI agent security, each one wired directly into an incident the moment it fires. An AI analyst investigates every single one immediately, corroborating the signal against independent evidence and returning a verdict with citations a human can act on or audit in seconds. Multi event correlation catches the sequences and patterns a single rule would miss. Threat intelligence and identity enrichment happen now an event lands, not hours later during a manual review. And when five detections fire from one root cause, they collapse into a single case instead of paging an analyst five separate times.
          </p>

